   "id": "np9-2.2-005",
   "obj": "2.2",
   "diff": 1,
   "q": "How does IEEE 802.1Q mark which VLAN a frame belongs to as it crosses a trunk link?",
   "choices": [
    "It wraps the frame in a new 26-byte header and adds a new FCS",
    "It writes the VLAN number into the DSCP field of the IP header",
    "It inserts a 4-byte tag with a 12-bit VLAN ID after the source MAC",
    "It rewrites the destination MAC address to a per-VLAN multicast"
   ],
   "answer": 2,
   "why": "802.1Q inserts a 4-byte tag between the source address and the Type/Length field: a TPID of 0x8100, 3 priority bits, a CFI bit and a 12-bit VLAN ID. Frames in the native VLAN are sent untagged.",
   "whyNot": {
    "0": "A 26-byte header plus a new FCS describes Cisco's older proprietary ISL encapsulation, not 802.1Q.",
    "1": "DSCP is a Layer 3 QoS marking in the IP header; 802.1Q works at Layer 2 and tags non-IP frames too.",
    "3": "802.1Q leaves the MAC addresses unchanged; the VLAN ID travels in the inserted tag."
   },
   "src": [
    "cisco-8021q-format"
   ],
   "checkOnly": true
