   "obj": "2.2",
   "diff": 3,
   "q": "Two switches are cabled with four parallel links for link aggregation. Both ends are set to LACP passive mode, and no port channel forms. Which change fixes the problem?",
   "choices": [
    "Set one end to PAgP desirable to start negotiation",
    "Set at least one end to LACP active mode",
    "Enable PortFast on the member ports of both switches",
    "Put each member link in a different VLAN on both ends"
   ],
   "answer": 1,
   "why": "LACP (IEEE 802.3ad) passive ports answer LACP packets but never start negotiation, so passive to passive never forms a channel. An active port starts negotiation and bonds with an active or passive partner.",
   "whyNot": {
    "0": "One channel cannot mix PAgP and LACP, so a PAgP port will not bond with an LACP partner.",
    "2": "PortFast speeds up spanning tree on edge ports; it does not start LACP negotiation.",
    "3": "Member ports must share the same VLAN configuration, so different VLANs per link keep them out of the bundle."
   },
