   "id": "np9-5.4-101",
   "obj": "5.4",
   "diff": 2,
   "q": "Calls between two offices over a WAN path have clear audio with no gaps or choppiness, but people keep talking over each other and pausing awkwardly. A ping test shows round-trip times steady at about 600 ms with 0% loss. Which problem is this?",
   "choices": [
    "High latency on the path",
    "High jitter on the path",
    "Packet loss on the path",
    "Too little bandwidth on the path"
   ],
   "answer": 0,
   "why": "Latency is the delay for traffic to cross the path. ITU-T G.114 treats up to 150 ms one way as acceptable for most uses, and Cisco notes that past about 200 to 250 ms one way, talkers fall out of sync and talk over each other. Steady 600 ms round trips (about 300 ms one way) mean high delay, not variation or loss.",
   "whyNot": {
    "1": "Jitter is variation in delay, which makes audio choppy; these round-trip times are steady.",
    "2": "Ping shows 0% loss and the audio has no gaps, so packets are arriving.",
    "3": "Too little bandwidth causes congestion, drops and choppy audio; steady times and clean audio point to delay instead."
   },
